PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Round 0 split: majority (2,6,8,10,11) favor F via octosyllabic couplets with explicit pairs (wente/hente/sente, here/manere/there/clere, fool/hool/cool, hool/fool, countour/tour/honour/sour/labour/discountour). Dissent: 3/4/9 for C (fool/fol absent or non-rhyming; spelling note); 1/7 for B (here adverbial/no end-pairs); 5 for E. 12's "Chaucer's name" claim erroneous. Round 1: near-unanimous shift to F (all except 7, who leans F with residual "fool" spelling caveat). Dissenters concede attested variants (fol/hool etc.) place every item in rhyme position; no lexical gaps survive. Key preserved: couplet attestations vs. absence claims; spelling flexibility under ME orthography. My prior lean: F. Round 2: unanimous consensus on F (all 1-12). All cite same exhaustive pairs + ME variants; no residual objections or gaps remain. Evidence decisive; question resolved as F.
Round 3: full unanimous F (1-12, including prior C/B/E dissenters). Catalog now includes fol/hool + discountour/labour variants; ME phonology/orthography confirms every lemma in attested end-position. No disagreements, spelling objections, or positional gaps survive; earlier errors (e.g., Col12 name-rhyme claim) already corrected. Consensus stable and exhaustive.
Round 4: full convergence persists on F; all 1-12 now explicitly endorse the same verified inventory (including Cols 3/4/9/1/7/5 former dissenters), reinforcing fol/hool reversals, here/manere sets, and countour variants under ME spelling. No new objections; Col12 error remains corrected. Answer fixed as F.}

